{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"Let me start by understanding the current state. The task is Stage B of B-0035: implement host integration for complete_inventory in repository.py and navigation.py.\n\nKey requirements:\n1. The first RepositoryCodingHost model request must contain exactly one fully authorized resource inventory as separate metadata. The exact objective comes first and is present; no inference step for inventory alone is needed.\n2. Use the approved representation contract (the `complete_inventory` function in navigation.py — already exists, probably from Stage A).\n3. The complete inventory is not a ranked/incomplete task candidate view. Both must coexist when task navigation is enabled.\n4. Repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventories. After an approved source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\n5. The inventory does not disclose source text, unauthorized paths, or absolute roots; it creates no credit for source acquisition or edit evidence. Preserve exact source bindings, currency, retained native history, and custody. Inserting the metadata must not misalign source message or check feedback indices.\n6. Count the complete inventory in the capacity of the fully serialized ModelRequest (including Tools and other Context). If required metadata does not fit, explicitly refuse with a budget/capacity result before model entry; projection must not silently drop it. Preserve objective, native boundaries, existing compaction.\n7. Keep initial metadata and installed-source refresh behavior available without requiring optional task navigation to be enabled.\n\nEditable files: only src/devtools/agents/coding/repository.py and navigation.py. repository.py has only 737 bytes of resulting source headroom. navigation.py has substantial space.\n\nLet me look at what I've seen so far:\n\nrepository.py:\n- Imports from navigation: FIND, READ_UNIT, TASK_FIND, NavigationPolicy, acquire_candidates, navigation_request, prepare_navigation, task_selection, unit_read_request.\n- The `RepositoryCodingHost` class has fields objective, scope, corpus, messages, disclosures, source_results, active_disclosures, projection_outcomes, projection_error, _source_messages, _check_feedback, requests, responses, check_results, last_completion, and calls self._index().\n- `_index` builds the search index.\n- `build_request(messages)` assembles a ModelRequest with Prompt(\"messages\", messages) and tools (_READ, _EDIT, _CHECK, plus TASK_FIND/FIND + READ_UNIT if navigation).\n- `prepare(progress)`: sets progress, scope.check_current(), prepare_navigation(self.messages, self.index, self.objective.text, self.navigation_policy), request = self.build_request(tuple(self.messages)), then projection logic: if self.projection is not None: check unacknowledged effects, compute protected indices (check_feedback + source messages with current snapshot id when readiness is not None; plus compaction.protect), project_request(...). If outcome.status == \"insufficient\": raise ProjectionCapacityError(outcome). Then request = outcome.request, active_disclosures = outcome.active_sources. Otherwise if request_bytes(request) > self.objective.prompt_bytes: raise ValueError(\"Coding Prompt byte budget exceeded.\"). Append to requests and return.\n\n- `apply(proposal)`: after executing the edit call, observes the new scope, verifies expected content, sets self.scope = observed, self.active_disclosures = (), self._index(), self.disclosures = [], then materializes disclosure for each changed resource.\n\nnavigation.py:\n- `prepare_navigation(messages, index, objective, policy)`: if policy is not None and len(messages) == 1: appends a user message containing json.dumps(task_entry(index, objective, policy)).\n- `task_entry(index, objective, policy)` -> dict: \"selects file-first lexical candidates, not complete inventory or source.\"\n- `complete_inventory(scope, editable, maximum_bytes)` -> dict: builds metadata with repository_id, snapshot_id, source_acquired False, complete_inventory True, resources (address, content_identity, byte_size, editable), checks size against maximum_bytes.\n\nThe focused test's assert_metadata asserts data[\"repository_id\"], data[\"snapshot_id\"], data[\"source_acquired\"] is False, rows keyed by address with editable flag, content_identity and byte_size matching the scope snapshot.\n\nSo Stage A created `complete_inventory` in navigation.py (approved representation). Stage B needs to wire it into RepositoryCodingHost: the first model request must include exactly one complete inventory as separate metadata — probably a user message after the objective message? \"The exact objective comes first and is present\" — messages[0] is the objective text. Then append the inventory JSON as a separate message (user role). And when task navigation is enabled, prepare_navigation appends the task_entry; both must coexist.\n\nKey details:\n- \"Repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventories.\" So we need to track whether the inventory has already been inserted for this scope. E.g., a flag or storing snapshot id of the last inserted inventory. After apply(), scope changes, so the next request's inventory describes the new scope — i.e., re-insert (or replace) with fresh metadata. \"After an approved source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\"\n\n- \"Inserting the metadata must not misalign source message or check feedback indices.\" The host tracks _source_messages: dict[int, tuple[SourceRangeDisclosureOption, ...]] keyed by message index, and _check_feedback: set[int] of message indices. If we insert a new message at position 1 (after objective), subsequent indices shift. Hmm — but when is the inventory inserted? At prepare time. Source messages are added when model calls read tool... Let me think about how source results get appended to messages. I haven't seen that part yet. There must be code that appends feedback messages after tool execution and records _source_messages[len(self.messages) - 1] = ... etc.\n\nActually, wait — the inventory is inserted as a message into self.messages. If it's inserted at index 1 on the first prepare, then all subsequent appends (tool results, check feedback) go to indices ≥ 2. The indices in _source_messages/_check_feedback are computed from len(self.messages) at append time, so they remain consistent with final message list as long as insertion happens before those appends and is idempotent.\n\nBut \"repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventories\" — the inventory message must be inserted only once per scope (or replaced when scope changes). If scope changes after apply, the old inventory message in history becomes stale... \"the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\" Hmm, does this mean replace the existing inventory message in-place, or remove and re-add? If we replace in place at index 1, indices don't shift. That seems cleanest: keep a dedicated slot.\n\nBut wait — what about \"retained native history\"? Native history includes tool call/result messages. The inventory message is host metadata, not native history. Replacing it in place preserves all other indices.\n\nAlternatively, we could track the inventory as part of build_request rather than self.messages... but prepare_navigation appends task_entry to self.messages when len(messages)==1. Hmm, if I insert inventory at index 1 first, then len(messages) becomes 2 and prepare_navigation's condition `len(messages) == 1` fails! That breaks task navigation coexistence.\n\nSo order matters: in prepare(), currently:\n```\nself.scope.check_current()\nprepare_navigation(self.messages, self.index, self.objective.text, self.navigation_policy)\nrequest = self.build_request(tuple(self.messages))\n```\n\nIf I insert inventory before prepare_navigation, len(messages)==2 and task entry won't be appended. So either:\n1. Modify prepare_navigation's condition to check \"no non-objective messages yet\" in a different way — but navigation.py is editable so we can change prepare_navigation. But careful: \"do not change... approved Stage A behavior.\" Stage A behavior includes complete_inventory contract and probably task_entry. Changing prepare_navigation's append condition may be permissible if it preserves the observable behavior (task entry appended exactly once when enabled).\n\n2. Insert inventory after prepare_navigation, at index 1 (before the task entry message) or at end. If inserted at index 1 after task entry appended, then messages = [objective, inventory, task_entry]. Task entry is \"a ranked/incomplete task candidate view\" — coexistence is fine either way.\n\nBut wait: does anything else depend on len(self.messages) == 1? Let me search for other uses. Also, the condition for inserting inventory must be idempotent across repeated prepare calls: e.g., track `self._inventory_snapshot_id` (snapshot id of scope when last inserted). Insert/replace when current snapshot id differs from recorded one.\n\nHmm, but \"repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventories\" — if we replace in place at a fixed index, no accumulation. And after apply(), scope changes → snapshot id changes → next prepare replaces the content of the inventory message with fresh metadata for new scope. This satisfies \"the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\"\n\nNow, where exactly to insert? If we always maintain invariant: messages[0] = objective; if navigation enabled, task entry is... hmm. Let me think about the cleanest approach:\n\nOption A: In prepare(), after check_current and before/after prepare_navigation:\n```python\nself._ensure_inventory()\nprepare_navigation(...)\nrequest = self.build_request(tuple(self.messages))\n```\nwhere _ensure_inventory inserts at index 1 if not present for current snapshot. But then prepare_navigation's len(messages)==1 fails when navigation enabled. So we need to adjust prepare_navigation's condition, e.g., `len(messages) == 2 and messages[1] is inventory`... that's fragile.\n\nOption B: Change prepare_navigation's guard from `len(messages) == 1` to something like \"no task entry yet\" — but how does it know? It only receives messages. We could pass an additional flag, or check the content of messages[1]? Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe better: insert inventory at end of messages (append), not index 1. Then prepare_navigation's len==1 condition still works if we call prepare_navigation first, then append inventory. Order in prepare():\n```python\nself.scope.check_current()\nprepare_navigation(self.messages, ...)   # appends task entry if enabled and only objective present\nself._ensure_inventory()                # appends/replaces inventory message\nrequest = self.build_request(tuple(self.messages))\n```\nWith navigation: messages = [objective, task_entry, inventory]. Without: [objective, inventory].\n\n\"Keep the exact objective first and present\" — satisfied (index 0). \"Exactly one complete authorized resource inventory as separate metadata\" — a separate message containing JSON of complete_inventory.\n\nBut for repeated prepare calls: _ensure_inventory must not append again. And after apply(), scope changes; old inventory message is stale. Should we replace in place or append new? If we append new, history contains two inventories (old + new) — \"repeated prepare calls do not accumulate duplicate inventories\" refers to duplicates; but stale inventory from old scope remains in history. Is that a problem? \"After an approved source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope, not cached old metadata.\" It says the next request's inventory describes the current scope — if we append new, the latest one does describe it, but the old stale one remains in retained history. Hmm. Stale metadata in history could be considered misleading (old content_identity for edited files). Also \"the inventory does not disclose source text... preserves exact source bindings, currency...\" — a stale inventory row with old content identity for a changed file is wrong metadata about current scope.\n\nI think replacing in place is the correct semantics: exactly one inventory message, always reflecting the current scope. Let me design:\n\nIn RepositoryCodingHost.__init__ (or state init), add `self._inventory_index: int | None = None` or track by snapshot id: `self._inventory_snapshot_id: str | None = None`.\n\n_ensure_inventory():\n```python\ndef _ensure_inventory(self) -> None:\n    current = self.scope.snapshot.id  # hmm, what type? snapshot.id\n    if self._inventory_index is not None and self._inventory_snapshot_id == current:\n        return\n    data = complete_inventory(\n        self.scope,\n        self.objective.editable_addresses? ,\n        some byte bound,\n    )\n    payload = json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False)\n    if self._inventory_index is not None:\n        self.messages[self._inventory_index] = PromptMessage(\"user\", payload)\n    else:\n        self._inventory_index = len(self.messages)  # append at end? or insert at 1?\n        self.messages.append(PromptMessage(\"user\", payload))\n    self._inventory_snapshot_id = current\n```\n\nWait, but what is the \"editable\" tuple for complete_inventory? The host must know which addresses are editable. Let me look at objective: `self.objective` has .scope, .text, .prompt_bytes, .disclosure_bytes. What fields does the coding objective have? I need to read more of repository.py — there's probably a dataclass like CodingObjective with fields including maybe an `editable` or effect. There's `self.effect` used in apply (self._ChangeTool(self.scope, proposal, self.effect)). Let me look at