``\n\nCounting bytes (roughly):\nLine 1: `    def _install_inventory(self) -> None:` = 4+38 = ~42\n`        key = str(self.scope.snapshot.id)` ~ 40\n`        if self._inventory_snapshot == key:` ~ 43\n`            return` ~ 12\n`        payload = json.dumps(` ~ 27\n`            complete_inventory(` ~ 29\n`                self.scope,` ~ 24\n`                self.objective.editable,` ~ 38\n`                self.objective.prompt_bytes,` ~ 45\n`            ),` ~ 12\n`            ensure_ascii=False,` ~ 28\n`        )` ~ 9\n`        message = PromptMessage(\"user\", payload)` ~ 45\n`        if self._inventory_index is None:` ~ 41\n`            self.messages.append(message)` ~ 38\n`            self._inventory_index = len(self.messages) - 1` ~ 57\n`        else:` ~ 13\n`            self.messages[self._inventory_index] = message` ~ 56\n`        self._inventory_snapshot = key` ~ 37\nPlus newlines: about 19 lines × 2 (CRLF!) — the file uses \\r\\n. Each line ends with \\r\\n = 2 bytes. So add ~38 bytes for CRLFs.\n\nTotal ≈ 42+40+43+12+27+29+24+38+45+12+28+9+45+41+38+57+13+56+37 = 696 + CRLF 38 ≈ 734. Plus in prepare `        self._install_inventory()` = 28 bytes → ~762. Over budget!\n\nHmm. And the __init__ additions:\n`        self._inventory_index: int | None = None` ~ 46\n`        self._inventory_snapshot: str | None = None` ~ 49\n= ~95 + CRLF 4 ≈ 99. Total ~860 > 737.\n\nSo I need to save bytes elsewhere in repository.py, or move logic to navigation.py (which has substantial space). Better design: put the install/replace helper in navigation.py as a function that operates on messages list, e.g.:\n\n```python\ndef install_inventory(messages, scope, editable, maximum_bytes) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Install exactly one current complete inventory metadata message.\"\"\"\n    ...\n```\n\nBut we still need state (index + snapshot id) in the host. State can be two small attributes. Or... could we avoid stored index by finding the inventory message? E.g., scan messages for a marker? The JSON payload starts with `{\"repository_id\":` — fragile. Better to store index.\n\nAlternative: store a single attribute that is a tuple (index, snapshot_id) or None. E.g., `self._inventory: tuple[int, str] | None = None`. One line ~45 bytes in __init__.\n\nAnd in navigation.py:\n```python\ndef install_inventory(\n    messages: list[PromptMessage],\n    state: tuple[int, str] | None,\n    scope: SourceScope,\n    editable: tuple[RepositoryResourceAddress, ...],\n    maximum_bytes: int,\n) -> tuple[int, str] | None:\n```\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think about what's cleanest and fits.\n\nActually — maybe simpler: keep the entire method in repository.py but more compact, and trim existing code to make room? \"Do not change tests, lint rules, authorization, or approved Stage A behavior.\" Trimming comments/whitespace in repository.py is permissible (not test/lint/auth/Stage A). E.g., long noqa comments can be shortened? Risky for lint (noqa comments must remain valid — shortening the text after -- is fine as long as the code prefix remains). Actually, we could drop some redundant lines. But let me first figure out exact headroom semantics: \"repository.py has only 737 bytes of resulting source headroom.\" I interpret: the size limit for repository.py minus current size = 737 bytes of room for net growth. So my net additions to repository.py must be ≤ 737 bytes.\n\nLet me design minimally in repository.py:\n\nIn __init__ state block (after self._check_feedback or near _source_messages):\n```python\n        self._inventory: tuple[int, str] | None = None\n```\n~52 bytes + CRLF 2 = 54.\n\nMethod (compact, single line where possible but lint may enforce line length? There's probably a linter with max line length; keep lines < 88):\n\n```python\n    def _install_inventory(self) -> None:\n        key = str(self.scope.snapshot.id)\n        if self._inventory and self._inventory[1] == key:\n            return\n        payload = json.dumps(\n            complete_inventory(\n                self.scope,\n                self.objective.editable,\n                self.objective.prompt_bytes,\n            ),\n            ensure_ascii=False,\n        )\n        index = len(self.messages) if self._inventory is None else self._inventory[0]\n        self.messages[index:index + 1] = [PromptMessage(\"user\", payload)]\n        self._inventory = (index, key)\n```\n\nCount:\n`    def _install_inventory(self) -> None:` 42\n`        key = str(self.scope.snapshot.id)` 40\n`        if self._inventory and self._inventory[1] == key:` 56\n`            return` 12\n`        payload = json.dumps(` 27\n`            complete_inventory(` 29\n`                self.scope,` 24\n`                self.objective.editable,` 38\n`                self.objective.prompt_bytes,` 45\n`            ),` 12\n`            ensure_ascii=False,` 28\n`        )` 9\n`        index = len(self.messages) if self._inventory is None else self._inventory[0]` — that's 84 chars > 88? \"        index = len(self.messages) if self._inventory is None else self._inventory[0]\" = 8+76=84. OK under 88.\n`        self.messages[index:index + 1] = [PromptMessage(\"user\", payload)]` 72\n`        self._inventory = (index, key)` 35\n\nSum: 42+40+56+12+27+29+24+38+45+12+28+9+84+72+35 = 553. CRLF for 15 lines = 30. Plus blank line after method (CRLF) 2, and maybe before def? In class body, methods are separated by blank lines; add one blank line = 2. Total ≈ 587.\n\nIn prepare: `        self._install_inventory()` 28 + CRLF 2 = 30.\n\n__init__ line: 54.\n\nTotal ≈ 587+30+54 = 671 ≤ 737.\n\nBut wait — where in prepare to call it? Must be after scope.check_current() (so inventory reflects verified current scope; complete_inventory also calls check_current internally). And relative to prepare_navigation: if I call _install_inventory before prepare_navigation, then when navigation enabled len(messages) becomes 2 → task entry not appended. So call after prepare_navigation:\n\n```python\n        self.scope.check_current()\n        prepare_navigation(\n            self.messages,\n            self.index,\n            self.objective.text,\n            self.navigation_policy,\n        )\n        self._install_inventory()\n        request = self.build_request(tuple(self.messages))\n```\n\nWith navigation enabled: messages = [objective, task_entry, inventory]. Without: [objective, inventory]. Both coexist. ✓\n\nBut wait — is the inventory message at end OK for \"the exact objective comes first and present\"? Yes, objective is index 0.\n\nNow, does anything else in the host depend on message count or indices? _source_messages keys are message indices where source disclosures were appended; _check_feedback similar. When are those appended? Probably after tool execution in the run loop (which I haven't seen yet). If inventory is at index 1 (no nav) or 2 (with nav), and subsequent appends happen after, then indices align naturally. But subtle: if prepare is called again (repeated prepare), _install_inventory replaces in place → no shift. ✓\n\nBut another subtlety: what if the model has already responded and messages include assistant/tool messages at indices ≥ 2, and then scope changes (apply) — apply resets active_disclosures and disclosures but doesn't clear messages? Let me check apply: sets self.scope = observed; self.active_disclosures = (); self._index(); self.disclosures = []. Doesn't touch self.messages. So next prepare: _install_inventory replaces the inventory message in place at its stored index. All other messages (native history) unchanged → indices of _source_messages/_check_feedback remain valid. ✓\n\nHmm wait, but actually — is that what we want? \"After an approved source change has been installed and verified, the next request's inventory describes that current scope\" — yes, in-place replacement with fresh complete_inventory(self.scope,...) using new snapshot. ✓\n\nBut also: check feedback protection uses `self._check_feedback` indices; those remain val